ank" rel=3D"noopener"><span>Behavioral Design Patterns</span></a><span>?</s=
pan></h2><p dir=3D"ltr"><span>According to this Design Patterns Cheat Sheet=
, choose behavioral design patterns when you need to manage algorithms, com=
munication, or responsibilities between objects. They're useful for encapsu=
lating behavior that varies and promoting loose coupling between objects. <=
/span></p><ul><li value=3D"1"><span>Behavioral patterns facilitate code reu=
se, flexibility, and maintainability by defining how objects interact and c=
ommunicate. </span></li><li value=3D"2"><span>Use them to address scenarios=
 like handling complex workflows, managing state transitions, or implementi=
ng communication between objects.</span></li></ul><blockquote><p dir=3D"ltr=
"><span>We must choose Behavioral Design Patterns when </span><b><strong>th=
e Problem is related to Object Interactions.</strong></b></p></blockquote><=
figure class=3D"image"><img src=3D"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-conte=
nt/uploads/20240506173449/structural.webp" alt=3D"When to Choose Behavioral=
 Design Patterns?When to Choose Behavioral Design Patterns?" width=3D"1043"=
 height=3D"746" loading=3D"lazy" title=3D"Click to enlarge" style=3D"cursor=
: zoom-in;"><figcaption>When to Choose Behavioral Design Patterns?</figcapt=
