ystem-design/observer-pattern-set-1-introduction/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D=
"noopener"><b><strong>Observer: </strong></b></a><span>Observes and notifie=
s changes in multiple objects.</span></li><li value=3D"2"><a href=3D"https:=
//www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/strategy-pattern-set-1/" target=3D"_b=
lank" rel=3D"noopener"><b><strong>Strategy:</strong></b></a><b><strong> </s=
trong></b><span>Encapsulates the interchangeable algorithms.</span></li><li=
 value=3D"3"><a href=3D"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/command=
-pattern/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener"><b><strong>Command:</strong><=
/b></a><b><strong> </strong></b><span>Encapsulates requests as objects for =
decoupled execution.</span></li><li value=3D"4"><a href=3D"https://www.geek=
sforgeeks.org/system-design/state-design-pattern/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D=
"noopener"><b><strong>State:</strong></b></a><span> It Changes the behavior=
 of object with internal state.</span></li><li value=3D"5"><a href=3D"https=
://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/visitor-design-pattern/" target=3D"_=
blank" rel=3D"noopener"><b><strong>Visitor:</strong></b></a><span> It separ=
ates algorithms from objects.</span></li><li value=3D"6"><a href=3D"https:/=
